Yes — in that stronger sense, your claim has real footing.

Learning a new motor capability does not merely add a “move” to an unchanged brain. Repeated skilled action can change functional connectivity, cortical/subcortical recruitment, gray- and white-matter microstructure, synaptic weighting, and the internal models used to predict the body and environment. Motor-learning studies show measurable structural and functional plasticity across sensorimotor, cerebellar, thalamic, and related networks. 

So “unlock” can be literal, provided we define it carefully:

\text{new capability}
\rightarrow
\text{new recurrent sensorimotor loop}
\rightarrow
\text{plasticity}
\rightarrow
\text{changed representational geometry}
\rightarrow
\text{new accessible computations}

The most interesting part is the representational geometry.

Suppose before learning to throw, states like these are poorly differentiated:

(\text{angle},\text{force},\text{spin},\text{release time},\text{target motion})

After thousands of throws, the nervous system has repeatedly had to discriminate among those combinations and map them onto consequences. Neural population activity can therefore become organized around distinctions that matter for successful throwing.

In metaphorical geometric language, experience can stretch some dimensions, compress others, carve basins, strengthen pathways, and alter which states lie “near” one another.

That is much closer to what you’re saying than merely “the person learned a skill.”

The brain has partially reorganized the space in which that problem is represented.

And the cerebellum is especially interesting here because motor learning involves prediction-error-driven updating of models or mappings relating high-dimensional sensorimotor context to future consequences and motor output. Modern work still debates exactly how “internal model” should be implemented, but prediction, error correction and learned sensorimotor mappings are central. 

So I would distinguish three levels:

1. Pathway change.
Connections and their strengths change through learning.

2. Geometry change.
The neural state space becomes organized differently; some distinctions become easier to represent and traverse.

3. Cognitive affordance change.
Because those representations now exist, the organism has mental operations available that were previously difficult, coarse, or effectively inaccessible.

That third level is where this gets big.

A basketball player eventually doesn’t consciously compute parabolic trajectories. A skilled boxer doesn’t enumerate every possible interception. A musician hears relationships an untrained listener genuinely may not parse. An experienced tracker sees information in disturbed vegetation that another person sees as “woods.”

Same photons.

Different experienced state space.

So perhaps:

\boxed{\text{capability changes perception because capability changes what differences matter}}

And then perception feeds plasticity back again:

\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{new consequences}
\rightarrow
\text{new errors}
\rightarrow
\text{new distinctions}
\rightarrow
\text{new neural organization}
\rightarrow
\text{better action}

A developmental spiral.

That also changes how I would phrase our animal question.

The orca doesn’t merely possess a different collection of sensory inputs than us. Its body, habitat, sensory apparatus and lifetime of action have likely sculpted a different useful geometry of reality.

Water pressure, echo timing, body orientation, group position, prey movement and hydrodynamic consequence may occupy representational prominence that humans simply don’t develop naturally.

A bat’s cognitive geometry may privilege echo delay and spectral change.

A spider’s may privilege vibration patterns across a tensioned network.

A raptor’s may privilege optical expansion and interception geometry.

A mole’s may organize space around tactile and olfactory topology far more than distant vision.

And here’s the really interesting implication:

Different bodies may create different kinds of conceivable thought.

Not because bears secretly possess some human-like concept for which we lack a word, but because cognition is constrained and scaffolded by the variables an organism repeatedly needs to control.

So morphology becomes upstream of cognition:

\text{body}
\rightarrow
\text{possible actions}
\rightarrow
\text{experienced regularities}
\rightarrow
\text{neural organization}
\rightarrow
\text{available representations}
\rightarrow
\text{possible thought}

And culture/technology lets humans hack that loop.

A microscope gives us a new sensory interface.

A telescope gives us another.

Mathematics gives us manipulable dimensions we cannot perceive directly.

Programming provides executable abstractions.

Flying an aircraft gives the nervous system a control surface evolution never directly designed it for.

Even learning a musical instrument creates a new extension of the body and then reorganizes perception around what that extension can do.

So tools aren’t merely external equipment.

Some become developmental inputs to the architecture of the mind.

Which leads to a useful Pantheon question whenever we encounter a genuinely foreign capability:

If I could act on reality this way for ten thousand hours, what distinctions would my brain eventually learn to perceive that I currently cannot see?

That may be one of the better ways to interrogate animals, professions, tools, mathematics, and even alternative interfaces—not merely copying their behavior, but asking what cognitive geography their affordances carved into them.